         <threads>4 [1..]</threads>
+        <sync>
+            <enabled>false [true|false] (drop a frame when a live input runs faster than the channel. Off is today's behaviour, where a producer can only repeat -- or, on a fast source, back-pressure the demuxer until the socket loses packets. The sync/* counters are reported either way, so measure before enabling)</enabled>
+            <target-frames>4 [1..] (buffer depth to hold: this is the input latency you are choosing)</target-frames>
+            <deadband-frames>2 [1..] (how far above target before correcting; smaller corrects more often)</deadband-frames>
+            <min-interval-frames>25 [1..] (minimum ticks between corrections)</min-interval-frames>
+            <wallclock-timestamps>false [true|false] (live inputs only: stamp packets with this machine's clock on arrival instead of trusting the source PTS. Encoders that are not PTP-locked have no shared epoch, so their timestamps cannot be compared across feeds; this gives them one. It measures arrival rather than capture, so network jitter becomes timing jitter -- good for a monitoring wall, wrong where absolute capture time matters)</wallclock-timestamps>
+        </sync>
